ouple examples of tools that can  be used for this  so here's a  very simple example just you're trying  to predict one variable from one other  variable  ordinary least squares linear regression  is going to have parallel lines so you  basically take your  regression coefficient and then you have  a standard error around that so you can  use that to estimate quantiles depending  on the other covariates  and these are always going to be  parallel  if you want to get a little more  complicated you can use quantile  regression where the slopes can differ  but they're still always going to be  straight lines  if you want to get more  complicated still you can use  non-parametric methods like random  forests which  didn't do very well i think random  forests need a few more variables to  perform well  or tensorflow so this is a deep learning  kind of approach where you're going to  essentially identify different kinks in  the data but still going to be straight  lines otherwise  so we did to figure out what approach we  should use when we're  doing this for policy engineering  products  is use a real world data set so  in this case we have a survey of  consumer finances we're going to try to  predict the quantiles of wealth and like  any other  kind of machine learning  system you want to have some way to  predict on new data  and see how it performs here so we did a  pretty simple 20 hold out we could  probably get similar results from other  um kinds of  approaches  and we tried to predict wealth quantiles  based on 11 features  so these features were things like where  the income the age family size marital  status and different types of earnings  as well  and we predicted a few different  quantiles  so seven different quantiles are what  we're predicting and we do that with a  few different  methods so  um i didn't show it here but one of the  most common approaches for this problem  is um essentially matching  so if we go back to  this table here we might just say okay  what's the most similar looking record  in um this table and that table so we're  going to say okay 100k  zero kids i guess the most similar is  going to be this 120k  and you can do this  statistically of course  um  and automatically but that's going to  give you 900k where 25k maybe just grab  the 22k  so  um  yeah that's a very common approach  so we tried that we tried ols we tried  quantile regression and also tensorflow  and random force  and we evaluated with quantile loss  so what is quantile loss this is kind of  the key part of quantile regression um  quanta loss actually looks very similar  to something you may have  seen before if you're predicting the  median so  at the 50th percentile it's really just  mean absolute error divide by two  so 50th percentile if you're either  ahead or behind your error is positive  or negative it's fully symmetric  um  so this compares pretty similarly to  mean squared error which  is a very common loss function for  for example ols  it gets a little more interesting if  you're predicting percentiles that are  not 50  so for example if you're trying to  predict the 90th percentile the point at  which 90 of the observations would fall  below that  you're actually going to penalize  um errors that are too low  more  than errors are too high  and vice versa for  uh  low quantiles you're gonna predict  uh penalize low errors less for low  quantiles  so the idea is you have to  have different quantile losses for every  quantile you're assessing  all right so what did we find well turns  out random force actually did the best  uh by  quite a bit compared to the status quo  of matching and this kind of makes sense  because matching doesn't actually  predict  a conditional distribution it predicts a  single value  um so ols did better the matching  quantile regression did better still  tensorflow and random force were pretty  similar  it turns out random forest is actually a  lot easier to use because you can  have just one single model if you're  familiar with random forests it has a  bunch of different predicted values  which usually are just used to figure  out what the average is but you can just  use that to have an empirical  cumulative predicted distribution so you  don't have to do separate  models as tensorflow sort of does for  every specific quantum  so yeah random force did really well if  you also decompose this by each quantile  random force did better than all the  others on almost all of the quantiles  except the very top ones so  tensorflow did a bit better at  predicting the top but um  random forest was still  pretty  um  pretty competitive in that area and it  did better at the other quantiles  i will say i think we could definitely  do better with tensorflow if we put more  research into tuning it but as i said  random forces just  ends up a lot easier to use  all right so this is what  led to our decision to use synth compute  and synthempu really just has  one core function that is used uh rfmp  so this imputes data with random force  what it does is it takes a few  um arguments but these are the three  most  uh important ones  and these are very similar to  what other prediction  apis look like so you've got  your training data set on the y you've  got your training  [Music]  sorry training data set as x-train and  your training labels  and also your new variables for x  so what this does is it calculates a  conditional distribution for every  record in x new  and it's going to select a random  quantile within that distribution  so this is going to ensure  that you're going to get  a proper overall distribution  both on the app  aggregate level and also for individual  um  you know similar kind of records and we  actually have an option as well where  you can  have  replicates of the x new and it's going  to predict uh  multiple times  so you get the full distribution there  we also do something that um  goes beyond the standard rf compute  approach where you still you enter also  your target  overall  value that you want it to sum to  so what this does is instead of  selecting a random quantile it's going  to select a semi-random  quantile and this basically means it's  going to select from some variable value  from the beta distribution rather than a  uniform when it's predicting which  quantile to select  and it then optimizes this to say  what um  [Music]  what were we going to do in terms of the  average  quantile to hit that target  so one example is food stamps are known  to be under-reported in survey data so  let's say we know that food stamps have  80 billion dollars that are spent on  them every year but the surveys only say  it's 50 billion well if you're trying to  impute data from  from that under reported value to make  sure that it's 80 billion  there's still you you could just  multiply by either over five  but  there are real values that are  in the survey data that are more common  than others and that represents a real  um systemic feature of the program so  instead if you say i'm not going to just  select so that the average quantile is  0.5 which is what the uniform  description does i'm going to select so  the average quantile is 0.6 or 0.7  that  adjusting that number is going to get  you closer to hitting that 80 billion so  that's what rfmp allows as well  max we've had a question from lewis  he asks  did you try tensorflow probability to  check the distribution of results  no um we actually developed this before  tensorflow probability was introduced so  yeah i'd love to try that actually  um thanks for the suggestion  all right i think almost done here just  want to share a bit about where we're  headed um  so one of the things i  i think i  showed it here maybe if we go back to  policy engine  um we've we have this live in the  product in one place and that's land  value tax  so you can actually select  a different value for uh tax on the  value of land  and that's going to show up in the uk  impact and  those pages  so we do that by taking  our core household survey which is  um we're sorry the survey that has land  value which is the wealth and asset  survey  so we can estimate land value there it  also has some other  variables that let's match it to the  family resources survey which is the  core household data set we use in  policy engine uk  so we use some compute to to do that we  also use the the feature that i  described of um  setting a target  so we want to do this for  a lot more surveys for example the  household surveys usually don't have  data on wealth  land of course carbon emissions  consumption so we can grab from these  asset surveys and these are going to  differ by country  but we can grab it for wealth we can  grab  we can basically calculate carbon  emissions in the consumption survey and  then if we want to do a carbon tax  simulation we can do that in the  household survey as well  same with the consumption tax like a vat  we can also use administrative records  to improve the income in the survey so  for example the current population  survey in the u.s  um the income is actually top coated so  if you have  tens  of millions of dollars in income it's  only going to cap that at uh maybe one  million so we're  so we can use tax data to improve that  estimation  all right and you know where does this  go ultimately in terms of the product  experience well policy engine we want to  build an app  that is one vision where we have a much  more democratic participatory approach  to policy making where you open your app  and you kind of see  from policy makers like political  parties or think tanks or individuals  policy ideas that everyone has and we're  all having the same data oriented  personalized conversation around how  those policies affect both you as an  individual as well as society  and you can click in to see the all the  details i went through before  click into profiles to see what kind of  pro  policies individuals have put forth  but all this sort of unified and  enhanced by having more accurate more  robust data underlying it  so that's all i have if happy to take  any questions and yeah you can go to  policyengine.org if you'd like to try it  out we're at the policy engine  on twitter  and uh my email is max policy engine.org  great well um  thank you so much for the the  presentation today max  thank you very much it's great to be  here
 

